  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nA quiet Thanksgiving Day is unfolding across the MPX coverage area \nwith satellite imagery showing some upper level clouds mainly over \nportions of southern/southeastern MN and western WI stemming from \nactive weather ongoing in the central plains. This has resulted in \nfiltered sunshine and area temperatures in the upper 30s for most \nthis afternoon, with some low 40s both within the Twin Cities metro \nand in areas of central Minnesota where there has not been as much \ncloud cover. As the system in the central plains moves a bit farther \naway into the overnight hours, the clouds should also thin out \nenough to allow for some efficient radiational cooling as lows drop \ninto the teens across the area. Temperatures tomorrow will be \nsimilar to today, although we expect increasing clouds due to a weak \nfrontal boundary passing through, with low level WAA being the \nprimary driver behind the near 40 high temperatures. The \naforementioned weak cold front will progress west to east during the \nsecond half of the day, with light rain showers initially eventually \nbecoming a light rain/snow mix with some freezing drizzle also \npossible, mainly at the tail end of the event in western WI late \nFriday evening. The forcing is fairly weak with limited moisture \nsuch that accumulation of either rain or snow is not expected, with \nmostly trace amounts for those that see showers up to at most a \nhundredth of an inch or two. Still, just a little bit of wet \nroadways could result in some slick travel conditions very late \ntomorrow for anyone traveling home from the holiday.\n\nQuiet weather resumes after the departing front exits the area early \non Saturday with lows near 30 warming towards the mid 40s by \nSaturday afternoon. This will be followed by some seasonal \ntemperatures to end the weekend and start next week, with 40s once \nagain possible for the first few days of december with no signs of a \nsignificant stretch of cooler weather on the horizon. Likewise, \nprecipitation chance are largely absent from the extended forecast \nwith weak subsidence generally holding in the absence of any \nsynoptic scale forcing. Heights generally stay stagnant with only \nslight fluctuations due to zonal flow as well, which will keep not \nonly cooler arctic temperatures but also any active weather absent \nfrom the region.",
